      "id": "s14",
      "type": "DSA_SIGNALS",
      "narration": "Almost every binary search failure is one of these lines, so learn them together. Left equals mid plus one, never mid, because mid is already checked and leaving it in stops the range shrinking. Right equals mid minus one, for the same reason on the other side. The loop runs while left is less than or equal to right, with the equals, so a single remaining candidate still gets looked at. On very large arrays left plus right can overflow, so left plus the half-difference is safer. Four lines. Write them from memory. That's the whole surface. Get those right and binary search stops being the pattern you dread, because everything else about it is the guessing game you already knew.",
